WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.670 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the first people to spot the russian  invasion of ukraine  were actually on the other side of the  globe  before putin had ever said anything  about his assault a group of academics  in california  noticed an unusual buildup of traffic at  the border and concluded correctly that  the invasion had begun  how did they do it  using satellite technology  six months ago i left active duty with  the marine corps and joined a defense  technology startup in washington dc  i was surprised when they asked me  to help build a military space strategy  i didn't know anything about space  i could barely tell you the difference  between star wars and star trek  i did want to be an astronaut as a kid  but if you're afraid of roller coasters  you're probably scared of rocket ships  too  i did learn that summer that for the u.s  space is considered the ultimate high  ground and as a result we've already  militarized it quite a bit  above us right now there are enough  satellites to take a picture of every  square meter of the planet  multiple times a day  in 2023  the us is planning to field a  space-based laser  that's next year  but guess who else is militarizing space  russia  and china  as a marine officer i can tell you  we're in a dead sprint  and no one is settling for a second  but here's what concerns me  using space to find better ways to kill  each other  is easy  what's harder  is using space constructively  to lift all boats  and not just deep in great power  competition  space is supposed to be the final  frontier  so is militarizing the hell out of it  the best that we can do  maybe there's a better way  this past quarter i took a class called  climate ventures  where i teamed up with aeronautics and  astronautics students  to explore ways that space technology  can solve climate change  during our research i found this really  interesting company called ghg sat  they're using satellite-based radar  technology to visually identify  methane emissions across the planet the  first time we've ever been able to do  that  another company  another approach  sponsored by harvard university and  backed by bill gates  uses a different approach  they use space technology  to spray fine particles into the upper  atmosphere and scatter sunlight and  actually cause global cooling  that would be a game changer for global  warming  but climate change is just an example  i think that space technology has the  ability to solve many problems in many  industries  for the benefit of all of us  and it would be great  if commercial space was on par with  military space  but there's two problems with that  first  though space is technically infinite  we're actually running out of it really  fast  between 90 to 600 miles above the planet  is a sweet spot where 90  of satellites have been deployed  we call this low earth orbit  or leo  90 to 600 miles sounds like a lot  but in leo  satellites don't sit still  they actually move and orbit  so instead of picturing a dot that  surrounds the planet  you should actually picture  a ring that goes around it instead  in 2021 the un estimated that there were  7  500 satellites in leo  that's a lot of rings  it creates a real traffic jam or in this  case a space jam  what makes it more complicated  is that when satellites collide with  each other  or when they reach the end of their  useful life they actually get stuck  because we don't yet know how to remove  space debris  so  when broken satellites and debris are in  space and leo they create a higher  likelihood  for more accidents which creates  more debris  and it's a vicious cycle  it's hard to tell how much space is left  in leo  but we're definitely running out  this leads to the second problem  because space technology is so expensive  defense has an outsized voice  in how commercial space dollars are  being spent  sure  spacex is a hundred billion dollar  company  but when the government is spending 54  billion dollars on space per year  it's hard to ignore them in your  business case  military organizations like space force  and the national reconnaissance office  are also outsourcing their space  technology to the commercial sector  so even though it looks like  a lot of money is flowing into the  commercial sector into commercial space  it's often just a trojan horse for more  military spending  personally  i completely understand why the u.s is  rushing to military space and if i'm  being honest i understand why russia and  china are doing the same  but great power competition doesn't  benefit humanity  what benefits humanity in space  is combating climate change or frankly  any use of commercial space  so i'm here to sound the alarm  the world's militaries are filling space  up fast  and the opportunity to shift the  trajectory of the space industry is  shrinking  so what now  if you're someone who's relationship  with space is primarily just watching  guardians of the galaxy  you still have a very important role to  play  we need to demonstrate  both an awareness of  and a demand for  commercial uses of space technology  and not just for climate change  but also for agriculture  finance  urban studies  maritime activity  and even politics  space technology has come so far  but unless you're in the military  you probably don't know about it  but now that you all do  you can help amplify the demand  for commercial space technology  start  by exploring the imagery that's been  collected by satellite companies  any stanford student has access to the  historical database of planet one of the  largest space companies with nearly 500  satellites in low earth orbit in leo  and data going back to 1972  i challenge you all  to use this data  and show us  something constructive that you can  build with space technology  planet's historical database is  available to mit and asu students as  well  but i urge planet and all satellite  imagery companies to make their data  available to everyone an open source  imagine the scientific doors that we  could collectively unlock  by demonstrating a ground swell  of demand  and finding novel ways to use space  technology that no one has ever seen  before  you  can help ensure that the future  direction of the space industry is  equally influenced by commercial needs  as well as military needs and avoid  the over-militarization of space  i'll leave you with one last story  two days ago russia  severed the internet in ukraine  a classic military move to disable your  opponent's command and control  it looked like a crippling blow  but within 24 hours elon musk announced  that starlink a satellite-based internet  service provider  would now be online in the ukraine for  the first time  picture that  space  not just an extension of our earthly  conflicts  but instead a solution for them a way to  resolve what we're doing to each other  here  that  is the power of space thank you  [Music]

